>> title: Biryani
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: chicken, rice, indian, saffron, layered
>> counters: Curry House
>> aka: chicken biryani, biriyani, briyani, dum biryani, hyderabadi biryani, birani
>> pairs-with: birista, raita, mint-chutney
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 30 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the rice is boiled until it bends but still snaps, because a grain cooked through before the pot is layered steams to paste
>> step.1: marinate 2 hr
>> step.2: boil 5 min, until it bends but still snaps
>> step.3: soak 10 min, until the milk goes orange
>> step.4: layer the pot, meat under rice
>> step.5: seal the lid on and steam 35 min on the lowest heat

Marinate @bone-in chicken thighs{2 1/2%lb}(1.1 kg; skinned and slashed) with @thick yogurt{1%cup}(240 g), @ginger-garlic paste{2%Tbs}(34 g), @Kashmiri chile powder{1%Tbs}, @garam masala{2%tsp}, @mint{1/4%cup}(chopped), @cilantro{1/4%cup}(chopped) and @kosher salt{2%tsp} ~marinate{2%hr}.

Boil @basmati rice{2 1/2%cup}(500 g; rinsed until the water runs clear, then soaked 30 minutes and drained) in @water{4%qt}(3.8 L) with @green cardamom{4%pods}, @cloves{4}, @bay leaf{1} and @kosher salt{2%Tbs} ~boil{5%min}, until a grain bends between the fingers and still snaps in the middle, then drain it at once. Rice cooked through here comes out of the pot as porridge.

Soak @saffron{1%large pinch} in @warm milk{1/4%cup}(60 mL) ~soak{10%min}, until the milk has gone orange.

Layer @&(~3)marinated chicken{} in the bottom of a #heavy pot{}, the @&(~2)parboiled rice{} over it, then @&(~1)saffron milk{} spooned across in stripes with @birista{1%cup}(the fried-onion recipe) and @melted ghee{3%Tbs}(42 g). Do not stir it, now or ever. The stripes are what makes a plate of biryani come out white, orange and brown instead of uniformly yellow.

Seal @&(~1)layered pot{} — lid clamped down under a rope of @stiff flour dough{4%oz}(115 g; flour and water, no more) pressed round the rim — and ~steam{35%min} on the lowest heat the ring will give, on a griddle or diffuser if you have one.

Dum is the whole thing: the pot is shut, the chicken cooks in its own steam from below, and the rice above finishes in that steam rather than in water. Break the seal at the table. A biryani is not a stirred rice — the shop next door that sells you rice mixed with curry is selling you a pulao, and the difference is exactly this lid. Raita is not optional beside it.
